items=[
('Sign stimuli are selective cues.','Restricted features can release particular actions, but their effects depend on posture, territorial context, reproductive condition, and the response measured.'),
('Fixed patterns and feedback coexist.','Goose retrieval combines a stereotyped inward movement with lateral corrections dependent on contact and the object’s deviations.'),
('Continuation is not successful retrieval.','The inward movement can persist after object loss; completion of a motor sequence does not prove that the egg reached the nest.'),
('State and experience alter release.','Repeated retrieval reduces execution readiness, experience narrows object acceptance, and response selection changes near hatching.'),
('Interventions require matched controls.','Candidate-gene delivery changes aggression within individuals, but broad expression and overlapping final scores limit localization and generalization.'),
('Molecular responses have distinct time scales.','A five-minute challenge changes RNA and chromatin-associated marks over 30–120 minutes; these measurements do not directly establish a release circuit or memory.')]
